#: source/development/building.md:260
msgid "Editor integration"
msgstr ""

#: source/development/building.md:262
msgid ""
"The `clangd` LSP server is installed by default on the `clang`\\-based "
"`devShell`s. See [supported compilation environments](#compilation-"
"environments) and instructions how to set up a shell [with flakes](#building-"
"nix-with-flakes) or in [classic Nix](#building-nix)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/building.md:265
msgid ""
"To use the LSP with your editor, you will want a `compile_commands.json` "
"file telling `clangd` how we are compiling the code. Meson's configure "
"always produces this inside the build directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/building.md:268
msgid ""
"Configure your editor to use the `clangd` from the `.#native-clangStdenv` "
"shell. You can do that either by running it inside the development shell, or "
"by using [nix-direnv](https://github.com/nix-community/nix-direnv) and [the "
"appropriate editor plugin](https://github.com/direnv/direnv/wiki#editor-"
"integration)."
msgstr ""

#: source/development/building.md:273
msgid ""
"For some editors (e.g. Visual Studio Code), you may need to install a "
"[special extension](https://open-vsx.org/extension/llvm-vs-code-extensions/"
"vscode-clangd) for the editor to interact with `clangd`. Some other editors "
"(e.g. Emacs, Vim) need a plugin to support LSP servers in general (e.g. [lsp-"
"mode](https://github.com/emacs-lsp/lsp-mode) for Emacs and [vim-lsp](https://"
"github.com/prabirshrestha/vim-lsp) for vim). Editor-specific setup is "
"typically opinionated, so we will not cover it here in more detail."
msgstr ""

